Parentheses are not enforced: `parsePrimary()` consumes `(`, parses the inner expression, and then only conditionally consumes `)` without throwing if it's missing. This can silently accept malformed expressions like `(1 + 2` and produce incorrect results instead of a `SyntaxError`. Recommend throwing when the closing `)` is absent (and similarly enforcing closing `]`/`)` in array literals and call argument lists).
```suggestion
      if (this.peek() !== ')') {
        throw new SyntaxError(
          `Expected ")" to match "(" before position ${this.pos}, found "${this.peek()}" at position ${this.pos}`
        );
      }
      this.pos++;
```

`parseNumber()` consumes any run of digits and dots (`/[\d.]/`) and then `parseFloat()`s it. This accepts invalid literals like `1.2.3` without error (and can swallow trailing dots). Consider implementing a stricter numeric lexer (single decimal point, optional exponent) and throwing `SyntaxError` on malformed numbers so expression failures are deterministic.
```suggestion
    let hasDigits = false;

    // Integer part
    while (this.pos < this.source.length && /\d/.test(this.source[this.pos])) {
      hasDigits = true;
      this.pos++;
    }

    // Optional fractional part
    if (this.source[this.pos] === '.') {
      this.pos++; // consume decimal point
      // Digits after the decimal point
      while (this.pos < this.source.length && /\d/.test(this.source[this.pos])) {
        hasDigits = true;
        this.pos++;
      }
    }

    // Optional exponent part: e+5, E-3
    if (/[eE]/.test(this.source[this.pos] ?? '')) {
      this.pos++; // consume 'e' or 'E'
      if (/[+\-]/.test(this.source[this.pos] ?? '')) {
        this.pos++; // consume optional sign
      }

      let expDigits = 0;
      while (this.pos < this.source.length && /\d/.test(this.source[this.pos])) {
        expDigits++;
        this.pos++;
      }

      if (expDigits === 0) {
        throw new SyntaxError(`Invalid numeric literal exponent at position ${start}`);
      }
    }

    if (!hasDigits) {
      throw new SyntaxError(`Invalid numeric literal at position ${start}`);
    }

    const raw = this.source.slice(start, this.pos);
    const value = Number(raw);

    if (!Number.isFinite(value)) {
      throw new SyntaxError(`Invalid numeric literal '${raw}' at position ${start}`);
    }

    return value;
```

`compileExpression()` allocates a new `SafeExpressionParser()` on every evaluation call. Since the cache exists specifically to avoid repeated work, this adds avoidable overhead on hot paths. Consider instantiating a parser once per compiled expression (closure-scoped) and reusing it across calls (the parser already resets `source/pos/context` inside `evaluate()`), or otherwise pooling instances if reentrancy is a concern.
```suggestion
    // Reuse a single parser instance per compiled expression to avoid
    // repeated allocations on hot evaluation paths. The parser resets
    // its internal state on every `evaluate` call.
    const parser = new SafeExpressionParser();

    return (...args: unknown[]) => {
      // Reconstruct the named variable context from positional arguments.
      const context: Record<string, unknown> = {};
      for (let i = 0; i < varNames.length; i++) {
        context[varNames[i]] = args[i];
      }
      return parser.evaluate(expression, context);
```

The `Function` spy only traps `construct`, but `Function` is commonly invoked without `new` (and would be invoked that way via escapes like `String['constructor']('...')`). Without an `apply` trap, this test can miss `Function(...)` calls. Consider adding an `apply` handler to the Proxy (and optionally a regression test that attempts a `['constructor']` escape) so the suite actually detects indirect dynamic code execution paths.
```suggestion
      },
      apply(target, thisArg, args) {
        functionCalls.push(String(args));
        return Reflect.apply(target, thisArg, args);
      },
```
